WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.379
So to start with, you'll notice that the scene here

2
00:00:04.379 --> 00:00:07.419
is quite overcast blue.

3
00:00:07.419 --> 00:00:09.580
And a big reason for that is, right now, we

4
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:12.140
are not taking advantage of our volumetric fog.

5
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.939
We are simply just using our Rayleigh scattering,

6
00:00:14.939 --> 00:00:18.460
atmosphere scattering, and just a general fog.

7
00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:20.460
So to change that, it's really simple.

8
00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.660
Let's just go into our exponential height fog

9
00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.160
and check volumetric fog.

10
00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.579
Now, I'm going to just close our environment light mixer

11
00:00:27.579 --> 00:00:30.500
for a second to show you that the environment light

12
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.899
mixer is simply just a tool that's

13
00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:35.619
interfacing on top of all the other things

14
00:00:35.619 --> 00:00:37.299
that we're normally used to.

15
00:00:37.299 --> 00:00:39.939
So for example, if I click my exponential height fog,

16
00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:42.939
go over to my Details panel, you notice here

17
00:00:42.939 --> 00:00:44.500
that volumetric fog is checked.

18
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.380
That's simply all our environment light mixer

19
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.179
is doing is just toggling that.

20
00:00:50.179 --> 00:00:54.380
So if I go to our exponential height fog, turn it off.

21
00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.979
See down here, it's off.

22
00:00:56.979 --> 00:00:59.380
All right, so we'll keep everything else as default,

23
00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.280
not the point of this section.

24
00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.390
We'll go in, we'll tweak it later as need be.

25
00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:07.670
One other shortcut that will help out

26
00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:09.189
a lot with adjusting your lighting

27
00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:12.629
is if you press Control-L, you'll get this little gizmo.

28
00:01:12.629 --> 00:01:15.349
And I just move my mouse around.

29
00:01:15.349 --> 00:01:17.109
So I'm not clicking anything on the mouse.

30
00:01:17.109 --> 00:01:20.359
I'm just simply holding Control-L.

31
00:01:20.359 --> 00:01:24.079
This is going to allow me to move the directional light

32
00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:25.040
in our scene.

33
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.840
And because we use the environment light mixer

34
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.319
to start with, all of our other components

35
00:01:30.319 --> 00:01:32.879
are essentially linked together in the background.

36
00:01:32.879 --> 00:01:35.840
So if I did something like kind of zoom up here

37
00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.200
so we can see outside of our scene, that's super blown out.

38
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.590
We'll go back in.

39
00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:46.430
But if I hold Control-L and kind of move this down

40
00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:48.030
to do like a sunset, you'll start

41
00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:52.349
to see that the coloration of the sky and the sun

42
00:01:52.349 --> 00:01:55.670
kind of changes to more of like a sunset or sunrise.

43
00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:58.989
So hold down Control-L and adjust your settings

44
00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.629
and just get something that looks good.

45
00:02:01.629 --> 00:02:03.540
Kind of put it here.

46
00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.700
And we are good to go.

47
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.139
So now what I want to do is just adjust some of our height

48
00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:11.979
fog to show you the different settings and parameters

49
00:02:11.979 --> 00:02:13.659
that you'll commonly use when

50
00:02:13.659 --> 00:02:16.099
needing to adjust it to whatever visual style

51
00:02:16.099 --> 00:02:17.580
that you want.

52
00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.620
So the first setting that we're going to do,

53
00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.500
so this is in our exponential height fog.

54
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.289
Again, we have volumetric fog checked, is our fog density.

55
00:02:26.289 --> 00:02:28.770
And that is exactly what it sounds like.

56
00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:31.969
Now, at the course of as we're building these things,

57
00:02:31.969 --> 00:02:33.930
you're unsure of what a parameter does.

58
00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:36.050
Just simply highlight over it.

59
00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.289
So just leave your mouse over there for about a second.

60
00:02:38.289 --> 00:02:41.289
And it will pop up a little contextual helper

61
00:02:41.289 --> 00:02:43.330
to essentially tell you what that parameter does.

62
00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:45.969
So fog density is exactly what it sounds like.

63
00:02:45.969 --> 00:02:49.280
So you can see I can take this up and get more fog.

64
00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:54.319
Now, you'll notice that as I'm left click dragging this,

65
00:02:54.319 --> 00:02:56.319
it stops at 0.05.

66
00:02:56.319 --> 00:02:59.039
You absolutely can take this above that by simply clicking.

67
00:02:59.039 --> 00:03:01.599
And let's do something like 0.5, go crazy.

68
00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:04.719
And you see we get a lot more fog within our scene.

69
00:03:04.719 --> 00:03:06.599
So if you just click drag, you

70
00:03:06.599 --> 00:03:08.400
see that you can do that, left click,

71
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.759
and type in whatever you want.

72
00:03:09.759 --> 00:03:11.240
So I'll do something like 0.25 just

73
00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:14.800
to show this height fog fall off.

74
00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.199
Now, it's going to be a little hard to see within our scene

75
00:03:17.199 --> 00:03:22.240
here, but this is essentially how far vertically

76
00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.080
your height will be viewed.

77
00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.719
So you can adjust this up or down

78
00:03:25.719 --> 00:03:28.280
if you want the fog to be more of a whole scene

79
00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:32.280
or just isolated to something like a low laying fog.

80
00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.919
You'll adjust your fog height fall off.

81
00:03:34.919 --> 00:03:36.599
Now, we do have secondary fog data.

82
00:03:36.599 --> 00:03:39.080
And this essentially is just an extra layer

83
00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:43.639
on top of our fog to allow for some more artistic control.

84
00:03:43.639 --> 00:03:47.039
So for example, we could set our fog density, fog height

85
00:03:47.039 --> 00:03:50.840
fall off to something kind of low like a low fog,

86
00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:53.650
kind of a ground fog, make it super dense,

87
00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.169
and then use our secondary fog, which the properties are

88
00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.449
essentially the exact same, to do something

89
00:03:58.449 --> 00:04:00.449
more like an atmospheric fog.

90
00:04:00.449 --> 00:04:02.330
So that density would be lower.

91
00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:03.729
The height fall off, we'd obviously

92
00:04:03.729 --> 00:04:06.610
want it to be able to go higher within our scene.

93
00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:08.689
And then height offset, we could just

94
00:04:08.689 --> 00:04:10.930
raise that a little bit again to not compete

95
00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:14.419
with our low lying fog.

96
00:04:14.419 --> 00:04:16.980
Now, this may be a little bit difficult for us to see here,

97
00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:20.300
but fog in scattering color, I will just click this.

98
00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.500
And I'll do something like a hot pink

99
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.660
show you just so we can kind of preview it.

100
00:04:24.699 --> 00:04:28.060
So you can see that this will change the in scattering color.

101
00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:30.459
Now, be careful with this property,

102
00:04:30.459 --> 00:04:33.379
because we can also change the in scattering

103
00:04:33.379 --> 00:04:36.740
with our sky atmosphere, in particular with the Rayleigh.

104
00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:39.379
But know that you can individually control this,

105
00:04:39.379 --> 00:04:41.579
which is great if you're trying to do some pretty

106
00:04:41.579 --> 00:04:43.160
cool artistic effects.

107
00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:44.199
All right.

108
00:04:44.199 --> 00:04:46.600
Start distance and end distance, we're going to leave at 0.

109
00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:49.199
And that just essentially sets it at infinity.

110
00:04:49.199 --> 00:04:51.879
But if performance is a consideration

111
00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:56.120
or you're trying to just eke out a few more frame time,

112
00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:58.839
then by adjusting these, you can specify

113
00:04:58.839 --> 00:05:02.759
where the fog is viewed, both again from the in distance

114
00:05:02.759 --> 00:05:05.509
and the start distance.

115
00:05:05.509 --> 00:05:08.189
I'm going to skip over a few of these other properties.

116
00:05:08.189 --> 00:05:10.509
And we're going to go down to our volumetric fog.

117
00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:12.029
So again, we've seen the checkbox,

118
00:05:12.029 --> 00:05:15.029
which just simply enables the volumetric fog.

119
00:05:15.029 --> 00:05:16.430
Scattering distribution, now this

120
00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:18.709
is a good one if you highlight over it.

121
00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:22.750
This essentially just says in the camera frustum

122
00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:24.629
where that fog is going to be visible.

123
00:05:24.629 --> 00:05:27.350
So I'm going to take this all the way up to 0.9,

124
00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:28.949
just to show as an example.

125
00:05:28.949 --> 00:05:31.350
Kind of zoom over here where we've got some thick density.

126
00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:35.949
And if I start adjusting our camera, you'll notice that,

127
00:05:35.949 --> 00:05:37.949
and this may be visible on the recording,

128
00:05:37.949 --> 00:05:41.709
but essentially where the fog is viewed.

129
00:05:41.709 --> 00:05:44.149
Now there are situations where, again,

130
00:05:44.149 --> 00:05:46.350
if we think about volumetric fog

131
00:05:46.389 --> 00:05:49.310
as it is in the real world based on science,

132
00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:53.870
there's just particulate in the air reacting with the light.

133
00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:57.550
So if you're outside and it's a nice foggy overcast day,

134
00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:00.709
you're typically going to see that light permeate

135
00:06:00.709 --> 00:06:01.230
through it.

136
00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:04.230
So on scattering distribution, so if I highlight over this,

137
00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:07.389
you will see a distribution value of 0

138
00:06:07.389 --> 00:06:08.829
scatters equally in all directions,

139
00:06:08.829 --> 00:06:11.990
while 0.9 scatters predominantly in the light direction.

140
00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:14.430
So in order to have visible volumetric fog light

141
00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.310
cast from the side, which is essentially

142
00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:20.470
where our camera is aimed right now, you'll be closer to 0.

143
00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.470
So if I set this closer to 0, you'll

144
00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:26.029
see that that intensity stays pretty much the same

145
00:06:26.029 --> 00:06:26.550
throughout.

146
00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.079
So again, we'll take this up to 0.9.

147
00:06:29.079 --> 00:06:31.160
And again, this may be a little difficult to see,

148
00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:33.160
but if I'm facing my light, you'll

149
00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.959
see I get full intensity.

150
00:06:35.959 --> 00:06:38.939
But as I kind of zoom around, you'll see that it works.

151
00:06:38.939 --> 00:06:42.220
Just keep that in mind, 0.2 is a good value.

152
00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:44.660
And then I'm going to adjust the albedo.

153
00:06:44.699 --> 00:06:46.579
Again, I recommend just playing with these.

154
00:06:46.579 --> 00:06:48.589
We'll do something like hot pink.

155
00:06:48.589 --> 00:06:50.670
You'll notice that this is slightly different,

156
00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:52.990
that this is just the main fog color.

157
00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:55.870
Now, because we are using Lumen, it does contribute a little bit

158
00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:59.110
within the scene, but it is a slightly different result

159
00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:02.189
than our sky atmosphere contribution and our fog

160
00:07:02.189 --> 00:07:03.550
in scattering color.

161
00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:04.870
So just be careful with that.

162
00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:09.350
Again, if you want some artistic effects, feel free to.

163
00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:10.829
All right, so next one we're going

164
00:07:10.829 --> 00:07:12.230
to look at is extinction scale.

165
00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:14.790
So if I take this up to something like 10,

166
00:07:14.829 --> 00:07:16.990
again, we're going to start getting some artifacts in here

167
00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:19.790
just because, again, we're at a very performance size.

168
00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:22.269
So you're starting to see the volumetric fog, kind

169
00:07:22.269 --> 00:07:24.310
of the grid calculations.

170
00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:26.629
But what this is going to do with the extinction scale

171
00:07:26.629 --> 00:07:30.670
is just up the contribution of the volumetric fog

172
00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:31.990
from our lights.

173
00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:34.550
So default value of 1 is good.

174
00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:37.300
Now, pro tip on this one, I do recommend

175
00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:39.019
with your extinction scale, I would

176
00:07:39.019 --> 00:07:42.180
start with 1 on your exponential height fog

177
00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:46.379
because we can individually control fog contributions

178
00:07:46.379 --> 00:07:47.620
on our light actors.

179
00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:52.160
So for example, if I select our directional light here,

180
00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:54.759
and what I'm looking for is volumetric scattering intensity.

181
00:07:54.759 --> 00:07:57.399
So if I take this to something like 0.1,

182
00:07:57.399 --> 00:07:59.360
you'll see that we still have our overall fog,

183
00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:03.360
but the god rays, so the crepuscular rays

184
00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.680
that are coming here, if you're in scientific,

185
00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:07.000
you'll see that it's lessened.

186
00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:10.040
And I can control this per light actor,

187
00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:11.839
as opposed to, if I jump back over

188
00:08:11.839 --> 00:08:14.399
to our exponential height fog, if I

189
00:08:14.399 --> 00:08:16.920
were to adjust the extinction scale,

190
00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:18.600
this will do it globally.

191
00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:20.959
So just be careful.

192
00:08:20.959 --> 00:08:22.959
View distance and start distance, again, this

193
00:08:22.959 --> 00:08:25.639
is a parameter to help control the artistic effects

194
00:08:25.639 --> 00:08:29.560
because there are situations where it may be too intense

195
00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:32.759
or you may not want to see it as far as a certain

196
00:08:32.759 --> 00:08:33.360
distance.

197
00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.600
So you can adjust these, but for now, we

198
00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:38.080
will keep them as is.

199
00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:39.500
All right, a couple other settings

200
00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:42.299
that you may want to look into, in particular

201
00:08:42.299 --> 00:08:45.899
deals with crepuscular rays, also known as god rays.

202
00:08:45.899 --> 00:08:49.379
So to enable your god rays and or just some

203
00:08:49.379 --> 00:08:51.220
of the settings, we simply need

204
00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:53.220
to go to the light source, which in this case

205
00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:55.679
will be our directional light.

206
00:08:55.679 --> 00:08:58.679
And if we scroll down, you see

207
00:08:58.679 --> 00:09:01.000
that we have light shafts, light shaft occlusion,

208
00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:03.080
and light shaft bloom.

209
00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:06.000
So if I just check light shaft occlusion,

210
00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.440
you'll notice that things kind of die down a little bit.

211
00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:12.080
Now, to help showcase this on the video,

212
00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.440
I'm just going to go to my exponential height fog.

213
00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.360
I'm just going to take my extinction scale

214
00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:19.320
and just increase it to something like 5.

215
00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:23.240
Just so again, it's just overly pronounced in our scene

216
00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:24.919
so we can kind of see what's happening

217
00:09:24.919 --> 00:09:26.980
with our directional light.

218
00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:28.379
All right, so go back to our directional light.

219
00:09:28.379 --> 00:09:29.720
And again, I'll scroll down.

220
00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:32.379
I'll uncheck light shaft occlusion and then check it.

221
00:09:32.379 --> 00:09:34.220
And so what you'll notice is that now we're

222
00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:35.899
starting to take into calculations

223
00:09:35.899 --> 00:09:39.659
our shadow casting objects and where shadows are being casted.

224
00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:44.059
And we are occluding the exponential height fog

225
00:09:44.059 --> 00:09:46.179
information from that light source.

226
00:09:46.179 --> 00:09:47.580
So if I go back to our scene, just

227
00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:50.059
hold Control-L and adjust this so you can kind of see.

228
00:09:50.059 --> 00:09:54.059
I'm going to try to get some to peek through here.

229
00:09:54.059 --> 00:09:56.179
So if I uncheck it, in particular,

230
00:09:56.179 --> 00:09:59.059
pay attention to these areas kind of hovering over here

231
00:09:59.059 --> 00:10:02.620
with my mouse where we have a lot of our fog

232
00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:03.980
permutation in here.

233
00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:06.059
So we see a lot of the fog in our scene.

234
00:10:06.059 --> 00:10:07.059
We can see our light.

235
00:10:07.059 --> 00:10:09.980
So if I check this, you'll see that now it's

236
00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:11.500
basically occluding that information.

237
00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:16.820
And it's only showing this fog effect in the areas

238
00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:18.740
where the light is present.

239
00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:20.139
Now, something to take note here.

240
00:10:20.139 --> 00:10:21.940
So the occlusion mass darkness,

241
00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:24.340
think about this kind of like a opacity slider.

242
00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:25.539
Now, it's not opacity.

243
00:10:25.539 --> 00:10:28.820
But if I were to take the occlusion mass darkness all

244
00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:33.100
the way up to 1, and if I uncheck light shaft occlusion,

245
00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:35.940
you'll notice that nothing changes in the scene.

246
00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:38.899
So this is how you can individually control

247
00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:43.620
or artistically control how much of that fog is occluded.

248
00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:45.340
So if I go to something like 0, you

249
00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:47.019
see that it only shows in areas

250
00:10:47.019 --> 00:10:50.779
where the light is there versus 0.05, we get a little bit.

251
00:10:50.779 --> 00:10:52.820
So again, from an artistic perspective,

252
00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:56.259
if you felt like, hey, this is a really foggy scene,

253
00:10:56.259 --> 00:10:58.620
it shouldn't be this clear in my shadows,

254
00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:01.980
well, just take your occlusion mass darkness up.

255
00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:06.460
And you see we can add some of that back to our scene.

256
00:11:06.460 --> 00:11:09.220
All right, so let's go back down here.

257
00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:12.100
The other thing that we can do is our light shaft bloom.

258
00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:13.539
So if I check this, we'll probably

259
00:11:13.539 --> 00:11:16.899
get something that blows out quite heavily

260
00:11:16.899 --> 00:11:18.899
and very similar to other bloom properties,

261
00:11:18.899 --> 00:11:21.019
bloom scale and threshold.

262
00:11:21.019 --> 00:11:23.980
So if I take the threshold up all the way,

263
00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:26.620
may not be visible because this is very, very prominent.

264
00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:30.980
But bloom scale, I could do something like 0.1 or 0.01.

265
00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:35.100
It's very sensitive towards the lower end of the scale here.

266
00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:38.899
So if I take it like 0.2, and you say ramp it up,

267
00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:41.100
it's not a whole lot of effect outside

268
00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:42.460
of just getting blown out.

269
00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:44.220
But if you're really trying to lessen this,

270
00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:46.820
do something like 0.01.

271
00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:49.970
I'll uncheck this, check it on.

272
00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:53.950
And you see we just get some nice extra, essentially

273
00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:56.950
what this is mimicking right particulate in the air,

274
00:11:56.950 --> 00:11:58.509
light is bouncing off of it.

275
00:11:58.509 --> 00:12:00.470
They're kind of like mini bounce cards.

276
00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:03.710
So that light is bouncing off of that particulate

277
00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:05.230
and going back into our scene.

278
00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:08.269
So this adds a nice little touch of realism.

279
00:12:08.309 --> 00:12:10.669
But again, the default values may be a little too bright

280
00:12:10.669 --> 00:12:13.669
for you, so feel free to adjust that.

281
00:12:13.669 --> 00:12:15.230
And then of course, in tandem with that

282
00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:17.029
is the bloom threshold.

283
00:12:17.029 --> 00:12:19.590
So this is just something where you can control

284
00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:22.509
where that bloom will be visible.

285
00:12:22.509 --> 00:12:24.710
So we'll keep it at 4.

286
00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:27.299
I'm sorry, set it back to 0.

287
00:12:27.299 --> 00:12:29.559
And then bloom max brightness, again, this

288
00:12:29.559 --> 00:12:31.460
is just something we can adjust.

289
00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:33.279
Similar in nature to bloom scale,

290
00:12:33.279 --> 00:12:35.940
you'll see that bloom max brightness,

291
00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:37.379
the slider is very sensitive.

292
00:12:37.419 --> 00:12:41.100
We kind of go from 15, 20 to 100 all of a sudden.

293
00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:44.590
Again, you can click in here, do something like 50,

294
00:12:44.590 --> 00:12:47.789
or you can drag this as well.

295
00:12:47.789 --> 00:12:50.669
And then we've got bloom tint, which is just again another

296
00:12:50.669 --> 00:12:52.309
layer of artistic control.

297
00:12:52.309 --> 00:12:55.379
So I could do something like our hot pink.

298
00:12:55.379 --> 00:12:59.340
And you'll notice that this only affects bloom, not

299
00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:02.100
the actual volumetric itself, or the fog color,

300
00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:03.879
or the end scattering color.

301
00:13:03.879 --> 00:13:08.070
This is specifically just for the bloom.

302
00:13:08.070 --> 00:13:10.970
Reset that.

303
00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:13.929
All right, so that should cover the basics

304
00:13:13.929 --> 00:13:15.850
of editing our volumetric fog.

305
00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:18.370
Hopefully, you understand the parameters and the properties

306
00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:21.929
that you would adjust to achieve the artistic effect

307
00:13:21.929 --> 00:13:24.289
that you're looking for.

308
00:13:24.289 --> 00:13:26.129
So before we jump into our next video,

309
00:13:26.129 --> 00:13:27.490
I'm going to do this off camera.

310
00:13:27.490 --> 00:13:29.490
I'm just going to reset some of these settings

311
00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:31.169
just to get it back to a default state,

312
00:13:31.169 --> 00:13:34.370
as opposed to our overly blown out, overly artistic state.

313
00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:36.850
So you'll see the magic behind the scenes.

314
00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:41.379
And then we'll go to the next module in this course.

315
00:13:41.379 --> 00:13:42.720
In the next video, we'll discuss

316
00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:44.539
how to optimize your volumetrics

317
00:13:44.539 --> 00:13:46.639
and cover various CVARs that will

318
00:13:46.639 --> 00:13:48.659
allow you to increase or decrease

319
00:13:48.659 --> 00:13:51.779
fidelity over performance.
